      },
      "lineNbr": 52,
      "author": {
        "id": 1112784
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-02-23T18:59:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is this still the case?  It looks like UART3 is being initialized in board.c, should it not be?  Also, the changing by one of the UART name between the hardware names and the USB device names is a little confusing, I see why it was done, but it might make the code clearer if they matched.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 52,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 52,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "b7871c60a36ec55eff456a456c4f46b77bb01afb",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "82bb7df1_74d5ac62",
        "filename": "board/servo_micro/gpio.inc",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 52,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000104
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-02-25T05:38:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Currently UART3 is accessible by USB but isn\u0027t pinmuxed out. Depending on the reworks present on the servo_micro one of UART3, SPI, or JTAG can work. \n\nThe naming unfortunately is servo header naming vs. stm32 naming. I\u0027ve tried to keep it clear by specifying USARTn (stm32 device) and UARTn (yoshi header spec)",
      "parentUuid": "62a5a9c6_7e892850",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 52,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 52,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "b7871c60a36ec55eff456a456c4f46b77bb01afb",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "02e4ed24_f6366d3b",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/usb_i2c.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 154,
      "author": {
        "id": 1112784
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-02-23T18:59:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m tempted to suggest removing this and the USB_I2C_REQ_ defines along with the board enable/disable functions, enable management values in the state struct and enable management code in the deferred callback.  This existed in the USB SPI bridge driver because SPI can only have a single master, but the EC wasn\u0027t normally that master, it had to do board specific things to become the master (put the AP into reset among other things).  But in this driver there is only ever one master of the I2C bus.  And if we ever needed the functionality we could add it with a protocol bump from 0x01 to 0x02.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 154,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 154,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "b7871c60a36ec55eff456a456c4f46b77bb01afb",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
